{
  "dom_id": "step-e13237",
  "name": "Example: Completing a Proof Template",
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/7c038824055de69a2e084057d135c240186282f1a3b99df56c3bcff30487baf2.svg)\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/8e41d0bb7db6b4082545af777f0c4b5b9cc1553f984711cca9c3fe5204f27cbb.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/85643739553554c5202dee34f7bdfd6dc24fdb1a91c97bab4c7c6d896f6404fa.svg) we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/f121d0925e5ea4b82a833af0bc6c3ba71ae3e92638a3bb86bc1c18e1ad0dfbd1.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/651065534a79ffdae519fc80952ae9e9276f564fe4f0b67ac3ea6e82ba230d95.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/c9087f49732c3fbf2f8afea1754e8818005390d3b23834591146d661fbc1ffbb.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/faabfd9d8e604c1afccfedfd097a5159e142da64fbfc366de688a49f94e07552.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/fdc9818d7db8a0c4bf9706eee2174748b5bed4e2800c65b04919384e8ba2f254.svg)\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1},$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/dc5e51875adfb1639996b90e8abad40a47bba0cca651141e37c69171812f4232.svg)\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/5777930d3dbfa03ba641031d02e0c6ad29199fbb7798a1a9c1a5879699e00219.svg)\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275250/assets/27a0a393eb3c9b621a31a9a8c4a2162c2708565f2d7d4c5f69e65ff76bac5b41.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
